1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:09,149
All six instruction formats are now in place.

2
00:00:09,149 --> 00:00:16,423
This episode takes a different view, asking how the address an instruction accesses, or the target it jumps to,

3
00:00:16,423 --> 00:00:17,341
is worked out.

4
00:00:17,341 --> 00:00:23,066
These calculation rules are called addressing modes, and RISC-V mainly uses three.

5
00:00:23,066 --> 00:00:29,887
The first is base plus displacement, where the address is a register plus an immediate.

6
00:00:29,887 --> 00:00:33,728
That is how lw, together with sw, finds its data.

7
00:00:33,728 --> 00:00:42,744
The jalr instruction belongs here too, jumping to rs1 plus the immediate, so the new PC depends only on a register,

8
00:00:42,744 --> 00:00:45,633
not on where the jalr itself is.

9
00:00:45,633 --> 00:00:50,715
The second is PC-relative addressing, which adds an offset to the PC.

10
00:00:50,715 --> 00:00:54,250
Conditional branches, jal, and auipc all use it.

11
00:00:54,250 --> 00:00:59,848
The third is absolute addressing, which gives the complete address directly.

12
00:00:59,848 --> 00:01:07,666
For example, lui loads the upper twenty bits of an address, and jalr adds the lower twelve and jumps.

13
00:01:07,666 --> 00:01:13,336
In fact, almost every instruction updates the PC in a PC-relative way.

14
00:01:13,336 --> 00:01:19,007
An ordinary instruction adds four, and branches and jal add an offset.

15
00:01:19,007 --> 00:01:21,194
Only jalr is the exception.

16
00:01:21,194 --> 00:01:28,727
Pseudo-instructions have their homes too, where j is shorthand for jal x0 and is PC-relative,

17
00:01:28,727 --> 00:01:33,133
while jr and ret are shorthand for jalr.

18
00:01:33,133 --> 00:01:35,543
Let's work out some concrete offsets.

19
00:01:35,543 --> 00:01:42,384
This is the loop from the course notes, with an example address on every instruction, and beq is at 0x0C.

20
00:01:42,384 --> 00:01:47,401
A label isn't an instruction, so there is no Loop or End in the machine code,

21
00:01:47,401 --> 00:01:52,333
and the assembler has to convert them into offsets relative to the PC.

22
00:01:52,333 --> 00:01:59,688
In case one, beq doesn't branch, and the PC just moves to the next instruction, so the offset is plus four.

23
00:01:59,688 --> 00:02:07,731
In case two, beq jumps to End, where 0x1C minus 0x0C is 0x10, so the offset is plus sixteen, four instructions ahead.

24
00:02:07,731 --> 00:02:14,536
In case three, j Loop jumps from 0x18 back to 0x0C, which is minus twelve, three instructions back,

25
00:02:14,536 --> 00:02:16,900
so an offset can be negative.

26
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:20,572
Remember position-independent code from the last episode.

27
00:02:20,572 --> 00:02:28,303
Move the whole loop to 0x100C, and every address changes, but plus sixteen and minus twelve don't need to change at all.

28
00:02:28,303 --> 00:02:35,712
Conversely, if an instruction had a jump to 0x1C hard-wired into it, moving house would send it to the wrong place,

29
00:02:35,712 --> 00:02:40,033
because an absolute address can't survive a move.

30
00:02:40,033 --> 00:02:41,880
Now let's encode them.

31
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:49,942
First comes beq x19, x10, End, with an offset of plus sixteen, which we worked out last episode,

32
00:02:49,942 --> 00:02:52,545
so let's go through it quickly.

33
00:02:52,545 --> 00:03:02,201
Then we fill in rs2 as x10, rs1 as x19, funct3 as zero zero zero, and the opcode as one one zero zero zero one one,

34
00:03:02,201 --> 00:03:05,500
which gives 0x00A98863.

35
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:07,017
Next we encode j Loop.

36
00:03:07,017 --> 00:03:13,362
The j is a pseudo-instruction, really jal x0, Loop, which writes the return address into x0,

37
00:03:13,362 --> 00:03:15,293
so it is simply thrown away.

38
00:03:15,293 --> 00:03:18,466
The offset in the J format is twenty-one bits.

39
00:03:18,466 --> 00:03:26,191
Minus twelve is negative, so as a twenty-one bit two's complement number, its upper seventeen bits are all ones,

40
00:03:26,191 --> 00:03:29,733
and its last four bits are zero one zero zero.

41
00:03:29,733 --> 00:03:38,269
As usual the lowest bit isn't stored, and the other bits go into their places in the order imm[20|10:1|11|19:12].

42
00:03:38,269 --> 00:03:45,068
Then rd is x0, which is five zeros, and the opcode of jal is one one zero one one one one.

43
00:03:45,068 --> 00:03:46,957
The result is 0xFF5FF06F,

44
00:03:46,957 --> 00:03:56,022
where the FF at the start and the FF in the middle both come from the long run of ones in the upper bits of the negative

45
00:03:56,022 --> 00:03:57,733
offset.

46
00:03:57,733 --> 00:04:02,774
Line up the S format and the B format, one above the other.

47
00:04:02,774 --> 00:04:08,498
In both, the immediate is split into two pieces in the same places.

48
00:04:08,498 --> 00:04:17,333
In both, inst[30:25] is imm[10:5], and inst[11:8] is imm[4:1], so they mean exactly the same thing.

49
00:04:17,333 --> 00:04:20,246
Only two bits really change meaning.

50
00:04:20,246 --> 00:04:28,581
The bit inst[31] is imm[11] in the S format and imm[12] in the B format, but it is always the sign bit.

51
00:04:28,581 --> 00:04:32,141
And inst[7] is imm[0] in S and imm[11] in B.

52
00:04:32,141 --> 00:04:39,400
When the hardware assembles the immediate, only these two bits need special handling.

53
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:40,277
A quick quiz.

54
00:04:40,277 --> 00:04:47,231
If a program contains only thirty-two bit instructions, is inst[8] of a B-type instruction always zero?

55
00:04:47,231 --> 00:04:47,501
Yes.

56
00:04:47,501 --> 00:04:54,860
The offsets are then all multiples of four, so imm[1] is always zero, and it happens to be stored at inst[8].

57
00:04:54,860 --> 00:05:02,961
Offsets count in units of two bytes, so a branch can't move by just one byte, but with only thirty-two bit instructions,

58
00:05:02,961 --> 00:05:06,833
half of the encodable targets go unused.

59
00:05:06,833 --> 00:05:13,412
Last episode we worked out that the range of a B-type offset is minus 4096 to plus 4094 bytes,

60
00:05:13,412 --> 00:05:18,801
about four kibibytes either way, or roughly a thousand instructions each way.

61
00:05:18,801 --> 00:05:23,491
Ifs and loops are usually short, so this range is more than enough.

62
00:05:23,491 --> 00:05:29,933
But if the target, far, is beyond four kibibytes, then beq x10, x0, far can't reach it.

63
00:05:29,933 --> 00:05:35,657
The fix is to invert the condition, so that the branch only has to hop over a j.

64
00:05:35,657 --> 00:05:42,885
When x10 equals zero, the bne doesn't jump, and executes the j far, which goes to the distant target.

65
00:05:42,885 --> 00:05:47,822
When x10 isn't zero, the bne skips over the j and carries on at next,

66
00:05:47,822 --> 00:05:52,133
and the effect is exactly the same as the original beq.

67
00:05:52,133 --> 00:05:58,510
The j is a J format, with a twenty-one bit offset, reaching about one mebibyte either way,

68
00:05:58,510 --> 00:06:02,407
or roughly two to the eighteenth instructions each way.

69
00:06:02,407 --> 00:06:07,296
That is also why an unconditional jump uses j rather than beq x0, x0.

70
00:06:07,296 --> 00:06:13,815
The J format has no rs1, rs2, or funct3, and the bits saved give the offset eight more bits,

71
00:06:13,815 --> 00:06:18,666
making the range two hundred fifty-six times bigger.

72
00:06:18,666 --> 00:06:25,774
As we said last episode, auipc with jalr can reach anywhere in the thirty-two bit address space.

73
00:06:25,774 --> 00:06:29,328
There is another combination too, lui with jalr.

74
00:06:29,328 --> 00:06:32,363
Let's look at the absolute version first.

75
00:06:32,363 --> 00:06:39,989
There, lui loads the upper twenty bits of the target address, and jalr adds the lower twelve and jumps,

76
00:06:39,989 --> 00:06:42,800
so the target is a fixed address.

77
00:06:42,800 --> 00:06:50,070
The jalr uses the old ra to work out its target, and writes PC plus four into ra at the same time.

78
00:06:50,070 --> 00:06:54,892
So one ra is both the base and the place the return address goes.

79
00:06:54,892 --> 00:07:01,198
The auipc version is PC-relative, with ra equal to PC plus hi shifted left by twelve,

80
00:07:01,198 --> 00:07:07,866
so the target travels with the code, and stays correct even if the whole block moves.

81
00:07:07,866 --> 00:07:12,078
The pseudo-instruction call expands into exactly this pair.

82
00:07:12,078 --> 00:07:17,503
If we only want to jump away and never come back, the rd of jalr becomes x0.

83
00:07:17,503 --> 00:07:23,642
The middle register also changes to t1, so we don't wipe out the return address in ra.

84
00:07:23,642 --> 00:07:30,423
Note that the twelve-bit immediate of jalr is also sign-extended, so how do we split hi and lo?

85
00:07:30,423 --> 00:07:33,800
That is the same problem as li.

86
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:39,861
Let's practice last episode's lui plus addi with two problems from the notes.

87
00:07:39,861 --> 00:07:49,306
First, when the constant is between minus two thousand forty-eight and two thousand forty-seven, li needs only one addi.

88
00:07:49,306 --> 00:07:52,062
Exercise one is li x10, 0x87654321.

89
00:07:52,062 --> 00:07:59,366
The upper twenty bits, 0x87654, go to lui, and the lower twelve bits, 0x321, go to addi.

90
00:07:59,366 --> 00:08:02,659
The lui first gives 0x87654000.

91
00:08:02,659 --> 00:08:13,705
The top bit of 0x321 is zero, so sign extension changes nothing, and adding it gives exactly 0x87654321.

92
00:08:13,705 --> 00:08:22,415
For the machine code, lui is a U format with opcode zero one one zero one one one,

93
00:08:22,415 --> 00:08:27,301
while auipc is zero zero one zero one one one.

94
00:08:27,301 --> 00:08:32,966
That gives 0x87654537, and the addi is 0x32150513.

95
00:08:32,966 --> 00:08:36,074
Exercise two is li x10, 0xB0BACAFE.

96
00:08:36,074 --> 00:08:43,623
Following the same pattern, we would write lui x10, 0xB0BAC and addi x10, x10, 0xAFE.

97
00:08:43,623 --> 00:08:46,642
But there is a problem with 0xAFE.

98
00:08:46,642 --> 00:08:57,121
Its top bit is a one, so as a twelve-bit two's complement number it is negative, and gets sign-extended to 0xFFFFFAFE.

99
00:08:57,121 --> 00:09:04,066
So adding it gives 0xB0BABAFE, and the upper twenty bits are short by one!

100
00:09:04,066 --> 00:09:13,262
That is because 0xFFFFFAFE is 0xAFE minus 0x1000, so adding it means adding 0xAFE and then subtracting 0x1000,

101
00:09:13,262 --> 00:09:17,441
which takes exactly one off the upper twenty bits.

102
00:09:17,441 --> 00:09:27,389
So we must add one to the upper twenty bits beforehand, with lui x10, 0xB0BAD, and now the sum comes out as 0xB0BACAFE,

103
00:09:27,389 --> 00:09:29,000
which is right.

104
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:35,056
The rule is that if the top bit of the low twelve bits, which is bit eleven, is one,

105
00:09:35,056 --> 00:09:38,084
we add one to the upper twenty bits first.

106
00:09:38,084 --> 00:09:42,554
As a formula, hi equals x plus 0x800, shifted right by twelve.

107
00:09:42,554 --> 00:09:50,124
Splitting hi and lo for lui or auipc with jalr in the last section follows the same rule, and with auipc,

108
00:09:50,124 --> 00:09:55,666
x is the difference between the target address and the PC.

109
00:09:55,666 --> 00:09:56,750
A quick quiz.

110
00:09:56,750 --> 00:10:01,338
How many bits does li x5, 0x44331416 take once encoded?

111
00:10:01,338 --> 00:10:10,429
The answer is sixty-four bits, because li is a pseudo-instruction, and here it expands into two instructions,

112
00:10:10,429 --> 00:10:12,666
lui and addi.

113
00:10:12,666 --> 00:10:18,683
Finally, let's sum up translating between assembly and machine code in two checklists.

114
00:10:18,683 --> 00:10:24,349
First, assembly to binary, where the I-star is the shift format from episode ten.

115
00:10:24,349 --> 00:10:30,086
In step three, when converting registers, first change the ABI name into a number.

116
00:10:30,086 --> 00:10:36,733
So s0 is x8, written zero one zero zero zero, and t4 is x29, written one one one zero one.

117
00:10:36,733 --> 00:10:43,177
The reverse direction is the disassembly from episode ten, with its four steps listed on the right.

118
00:10:43,177 --> 00:10:46,666
It is handy to keep a 61C reference card nearby.

119
00:10:46,666 --> 00:10:52,295
Let's practice once with the J format, translating 0xFF5FF06F back.

120
00:10:52,295 --> 00:10:57,251
The opcode is one one zero one one one one, so this is jal.

121
00:10:57,251 --> 00:11:01,955
Cutting it as a J format, rd is five zeros, which is x0.

122
00:11:01,955 --> 00:11:10,860
Putting the immediate back together in the order imm[20|10:1|11|19:12], and adding the lowest bit, a zero,

123
00:11:10,860 --> 00:11:12,456
gives minus twelve.

124
00:11:12,456 --> 00:11:21,300
Put together, it is jal x0, minus twelve, which is exactly the j Loop we encoded earlier.

125
00:11:21,300 --> 00:11:24,821
The notes also have a whole-file comparison.

126
00:11:24,821 --> 00:11:33,784
The label main has no corresponding machine code, and the pseudo-instruction mv a0, a5 is really addi a0, a5, 0.

127
00:11:33,784 --> 00:11:38,185
The last line, call printf, compiled into just one jal.

128
00:11:38,185 --> 00:11:46,400
When the target is near enough, the toolchain shrinks the auipc plus jalr pair into one jal.

